From 9acf9dc2ff40026ecc38069bd0c77d262f2cdddd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 05:44:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(video): reference videos become reusable style profiles
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Make one like this" is how most short-form briefs actually start, and the
reference is usually a video the user can point at. Uploading one now yields
a saved style profile that steers topic videos.

The analysis separates what can be measured from what must be described:
ffmpeg and the transcript give duration and narration speed, and one vision
call over six evenly-sampled frames describes hook shape, scene count,
caption treatment, energy, and framing. The model never supplies the
numbers, so a profile cannot drift into invention.

What a profile deliberately does not carry: the reference's footage, audio,
music, or wording. The analysis prompt asks for structure only and forbids
describing the subject matter, so applying a profile cannot reproduce
someone else's content. References are uploads only — there is no URL
ingestion, so nothing is fetched from a third-party host.

Fail-soft where a blank is better than an error: a silent reference just
yields a visual-only profile (0 wpm), and a deleted or foreign profile id
renders the video without reference styling. Hard-fail where a blank is
worse than nothing: an unreadable video or an unusable vision reply is a
422 and the reserved caption unit is refunded.

Analysis is metered as one caption unit — it is a single text-model
completion. The upload is downloaded before funding is reserved so a bad
path never burns a unit, and the 8-per-workspace cap is re-checked under a
tenant row lock.

In the studio, the topic engine gains a reference-style picker next to the
brand kit; picking a profile adopts its caption treatment as a starting
point, and a captions-free reference turns subtitles off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
